  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 224 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nTroughing is now dominating the high plains with a well defined \nupper low moving through MT/Alberta this afternoon. With this wave \nwill come severe weather this evening/overnight in eastern North \nDakota and strong winds along the south side of the low tomorrow \nalong the intl border. Beyond these threats the 0.25-0.5\" rains \nnorth of HWY 200 tonight will certainly be welcome and beneficial to \neasing the worsening drought. It may even put a short stop to the \nongoing wheat/sugar beet harvest. More thunderstorm chances through \nthe later half of the week, some of which may be severe.\n\n- Today\n\nThe strong forcing of the 500mb in MT is already proving to be \nenough to initiate storms across eastern MT despite modest MLCAPE of \n500-1000 J/kg out there. As storms continue to deepen and grow \nupscale they will work into a more buoyant airmass west of the US-83 \ncorridor with sig wind potential across the western/South central \nportion of the state. Given decreasing MUCAPE and instability with \ntime and eastward extent the storms are expected to be on a \nweakening trend by the time they arrive in our area around 8-10pm \nand midnight in the RRV. This comes with a caveat however, many CAMS \nare depicting a bubble of MUCAPE exceeding 3000 j/kg in the southern \nRRV that should storms enter it with sufficient organization they \nmay intensify supporting 60-70mph gusts along and south of HWY 200 \nthrough the early morning hours and into west central Minnesota. \nThis scenario is low confidence but higher impact and worth \nmentioning. Overall given weak low level lapse rates and minimal low \nlevel cape (0-3km) QLCS tornados seem unlikely but cannot be ruled \nout if storms remain more organized with a balanced UDCZ. Overall as \nlong as a linear mode prevails hail is a low threat.\n\n- Monday Wind\n\nAs the upper low lifts northeast into Lake Manitoba and Lake \nWinnipeg strong W/SW winds will wrap around the southern periphery \nof the low. EFI does pick up on this nicely with some shading over \nthe 0.9 threshold for wind gusts which seems to correspond nicely \nwith high wind warning criteria for us historically, anecdotally \nspeaking. Given a modest BL depth to around 800mb and flow within \nthat layer of 30-40kts only moderate cold air advection at the \nonset of peak diurnal mixing thinking that getting gusts from \nhigher aloft to the surface will be more difficult. The result is to \nexpect gusts of 30-40kts in the afternoon across northeast north \nDakota and especially the Langdon bowl but the peak potential with \ngusts nearing 45-50kts seems unrealistic. A few scenarios do bring \nbring much drier dewpoints that being shown by the NBM with \nresulting MinRH in the 25-30% range overlapping with gusts of 20-\n25kts (mainly in southeast North Dakota) with MinRH closer to 35% \noverlapping with gusts of 30-35kts in the northern RRV. Overall the \nstate of the fuels seems unlikely support fast fire spread but near \ncritical fire weather seems on the table. True Red Flag conditions \nseem unattainable at this time.\n\n- Work Week\n\nNear zonal to southwesterly flow aloft continues into next week.\nTemps look to be above average with several chances for \nprecipitation as weak shortwaves come through. Machine learning does \nindicate increased chances for severe weather but with a large \nspatial spread day to day lowering the confidence at the this time \nin impacts. Overall seasonal moisture and increased shear from the \npassing shortwaves amid SW flow do conceptually support the risk for \nstrong to severe storms through the work week.",
